  "items": [
    {
      "context_after": " On some bicycles there is only one gear and, therefore, the gear ratio is fixed, but most modern bicycles have multiple gears and thus multiple gear ratios. A",
      "context_before": "From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia A Rohloff Speedhub model hub gear A Shimano XT rear derailleur on a mountain bike A bicycle gearbox with chain tensioner ",
      "geography": "",
      "item_id": "ev_gears_definition",
      "locator": {
        "end": 1154,
        "kind": "char_range",
        "start": 970
      },
      "passage": "Bicycle gearing is the aspect of a bicycle drivetrain that determines the relation between the cadence, the rate at which the rider pedals, and the rate at which the drive wheel turns.",
      "quoted_at": "2026-09-26",
      "source_id": "src_wiki_gearing",
      "time_basis": "",
      "unit": ""
    },
    {
      "context_after": " Examplesedit The following table provides some comparison of the various methods of measuring gears (the particular numbers are for bicycles with 170 mm cranks",
      "context_before": "s the ratio between the distance travelled by the bicycle and the distance travelled by a pedal, and is a pure number, independent of any units of measurement. ",
      "geography": "",
      "item_id": "ev_gears_same_ratio",
      "locator": {
        "end": 6077,
        "kind": "char_range",
        "start": 5773
      },
      "passage": "Front/rear gear measurement uses two numbers (e.g. 53/19) where the first is the number of teeth in the front chainring and the second is the number of teeth in the rear sprocket. Without doing some arithmetic, it is not immediately obvious that 53/19 and 39/14 represent effectively the same gear ratio.",
      "quoted_at": "2026-09-26",
      "source_id": "src_wiki_gearing",
      "time_basis": "",
      "unit": ""
    },
    {
      "context_after": " Gain ratio is the ratio between the distance travelled by the bicycle and the distance travelled by a pedal, and is a pure number, independent of any units of",
      "context_before": "to one decimal place. Gear inches corresponds to the diameter (in inches) of the main wheel of an old-fashioned penny-farthing bicycle with equivalent gearing. ",
      "geography": "",
      "item_id": "ev_gears_development",
      "locator": {
        "end": 5600,
        "kind": "char_range",
        "start": 5483
      },
      "passage": "Metres of development corresponds to the distance (in metres) traveled by the bicycle for one rotation of the pedals.",
      "quoted_at": "2026-09-26",
      "source_id": "src_wiki_gearing",
      "time_basis": "",
      "unit": ""
    },
    {
      "context_after": " Conversely, a higher gear (smaller mechanical advantage) provides a higher speed for a given cadence, but requires the rider to exert greater force or stand wh",
      "context_before": "lso depends on the internal planetary gears within the hub. For a shaft-driven bicycle the gear ratio depends on the bevel gears used at each end of the shaft. ",
      "geography": "",
      "item_id": "ev_gears_trade_off",
      "locator": {
        "end": 2576,
        "kind": "char_range",
        "start": 2415
      },
      "passage": "For a bicycle to travel at the same speed, using a lower gear (larger mechanical advantage) requires the rider to pedal at a faster cadence, but with less force.",
      "quoted_at": "2026-09-26",
      "source_id": "src_wiki_gearing",
      "time_basis": "",
      "unit": ""
    },
    {
      "context_after": "[1] Measuring gear ratiosedit Methodsedit There are at least four different methods[2] for measuring gear ratios: gear inches, metres of development (roll-out),",
      "context_before": ", riding position, and pedalling force. Prolonged exertion of too much force in too high a gear at too low a cadence can increase the chance of knee damage;[1] ",
      "geography": "",
      "item_id": "ev_gears_sprint_rpm",
      "locator": {
        "end": 3056,
        "kind": "char_range",
        "start": 2972
      },
      "passage": "cadence above 100 rpm becomes less effective after short bursts, as during a sprint.",
      "quoted_at": "2026-09-26",
      "source_id": "src_wiki_gearing",
      "time_basis": "",
      "unit": ""
    },
    {
